# Far copy, step 26.6 (7.2.38, 7.2.44): the answerer with the check, the faults of the channel. One program of language 3, nothing new: data, array, lut, store, add, sub, eq, lt, if, key, emit, copy.
# It is far_serve.tw (26.3) with a checked protocol; far_serve.tw is kept as it was: the pass l4c (far_copy.tw: `copy ... 0x2...`) makes the requests of that protocol (6 bytes, no check),
# so the answerer of the checked protocol is a program of its own (the asker of it is far_get_ck.tw). Its window is the same: 8 cells of 8 bits, COPYMACH.
# The record of a request: 7 bytes, read by four `key`:   op (8 bits)   pos (32 bits, the number of the byte)   dat (8 bits)   ck (8 bits)
#   ck = op + pos[0:8] + pos[8:16] + pos[16:24] + pos[24:32] + dat, modulo 256 (five `add`). A byte changed in the record changes the sum: the fault is seen.
#   op = 1 (bit 0) get      op = 2 (bit 1) put      op = 0 and the record whole and right (ck = 0 for the record of zeros): stop (no answer).
# The answer to every record is two bytes, always (so the asker never waits for more or less):   v (8 bits)   w = 255 - v (8 bits, the complement of v: `sub`)
#   get: v = win[pos] (outside the window, pos >= 8: 255, w = 0: the prohibitive price is an answer as right as any); put: win[pos] = dat, and the answer is the cell read back (v = dat, w = ~dat;
#   outside the window nothing is written and the answer is 255, 0): the put is confirmed by what the window holds now. A record with a wrong ck (or an op not 0, 1, 2 with a right ck)
#   changes nothing and is answered by the two bytes 0, 0: they are never a pair (0 and 255 - 0 = 255), so the asker sees it and asks again.
#   The requests are idempotent (7.2.38): the same get gives the same answer, the same put leaves the same window: a repeat is harmless.
#   The end of the stdin (the channel is closed) is a stop: the whole record is zeroed before every read and a read at EOF leaves it as it was (all zeros: the sum 0 = ck 0, op 0).
# The sum goes through the cells s1..s4, s (every `add` has a result cell of its own): `add` into one of its own arguments loses the carry between the nibbles (the carry bit lands on a bit still to be read).
:op data 8 0
:pos data 32 0
:dat data 8 0
:ck data 8 0
:s1 data 9 0
:s2 data 9 0
:s3 data 9 0
:s4 data 9 0
:s data 9 0
:e data 2 0
:ans data 8 0
:inv data 9 0
:zero8 data 8 0
:zero32 data 32 0
:ones data 8 255
:eight data 32 8
:c3 data 9 0
:win array 3 8
:init data 64 "COPYMACH"
:i0 data 3 0
:i1 data 3 1
:i2 data 3 2
:i3 data 3 3
:i4 data 3 4
:i5 data 3 5
:i6 data 3 6
:i7 data 3 7
:main block
store &win &i0 &init[0:8]
store &win &i1 &init[8:16]
store &win &i2 &init[16:24]
store &win &i3 &init[24:32]
store &win &i4 &init[32:40]
store &win &i5 &init[40:48]
store &win &i6 &init[48:56]
store &win &i7 &init[56:64]
goto &loop
:loop block
copy &op &zero8
copy &pos &zero32
copy &dat &zero8
copy &ck &zero8
key &op
key &pos
key &dat
key &ck
goto &verify
:verify block
add &op &pos[0:8] &s1
add &s1[0:8] &pos[8:16] &s2
add &s2[0:8] &pos[16:24] &s3
add &s3[0:8] &pos[24:32] &s4
add &s4[0:8] &dat &s
eq &s[0:8] &ck &e
if &e[1] &good &bad
:bad block
emit &zero8
emit &zero8
goto &loop
:good block
lt &pos &eight &c3
if &op[0] &get &notget
:get block
if &c3[8] &getin &getout
:getin block
lut &win &pos[0:3] &ans
sub &ones &ans &inv
emit &ans
emit &inv[0:8]
goto &loop
:getout block
emit &ones
emit &zero8
goto &loop
:notget block
if &op[1] &put &notput
:put block
if &c3[8] &putin &getout
:putin block
store &win &pos[0:3] &dat
goto &getin
:notput block
eq &op &zero8 &e
if &e[1] &done &bad
:done block
halt
